@id     04_arrays
@title  Arrays
@topic  arrays
@lang   c
@show   values

@theory
An array stores several values of the same type next to each other in memory.

    int a[5];        // indexes 0, 1, 2, 3, 4
    a[0] = 7;

The last valid index is size - 1. Reading a[5] here is a bug: it touches memory
that does not belong to the array. CodeLens builds your code with AddressSanitizer,
so this kind of bug is reported instead of silently giving garbage.

Your task: read n (1..100), then n integers, and print the largest one.

Input:   4
         3 9 -2 5
Output:  9

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    int a[100];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &a[i]);
    }

    // find the largest element

    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    int a[100];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) scanf("%d", &a[i]);
    int best = a[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
        if (a[i] > best) best = a[i];
    printf("%d\n", best);
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
4
3 9 -2 5
@expected
9

@test
@input
3
-5 -1 -7
@expected
-1

@hidden_test
@input
1
42
@expected
42

#! ------------------------------------------------------------------
#! Steps: Explore -> Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step explore
@title  Boxes in a row
@scene  array
@text
Move the slider and watch which box the arrow p points to.
An array is several boxes in a row with one name. The boxes are numbered from 0.

@step watch
@title  Watch an array
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a[4] = {3, 8, 1, 5};
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        total += a[i];
    }
    printf("%d\n", total);
    return 0;
}
@text
Press Next line and watch total grow.
a[i] means "box number i of a". The loop visits the boxes 0, 1, 2 and 3.

@step predict
@title  Predict a box
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a[4] = {3, 8, 1, 5};
    printf("%d\n", a[2]);
    return 0;
}
@line   4
@expr   a[2]
@text
What is in a[2] after line 4 runs?
Remember: numbering starts at 0.

@step choose
@title  The last box
@code
int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
@text
Which is the last box of this array?
@option a[5]
@option a[4]
@option a[0]
@option a[6]
@correct 2
@answer
int a[5] has five boxes: a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3] and a[4]. a[5] would be outside the array.

@step find_bug
@title  Find the bug
@text
Fix the loop so it stops at the last box of the array.
Right now it reads a box past the end of the array, and CodeLens reports a memory error.
@starter
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@editable 5-5
@solution
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        printf("%d ", a[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
@test
@expected
1 2 3 4 5

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
Why is the last box of int a[5] called a[4] and not a[5]?
@rubric
0; zero; start; five
@answer
Numbering starts at 0, so the five boxes are a[0] to a[4]. a[5] would be the sixth box,
which does not belong to the array.

@step practice
@title  Practice
